1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,668
Your CTO points at a commit on main that
raised a rate limit in production and asks

2
00:00:09,708 --> 00:00:14,939
who made it. A commit is one saved snapshot
of the project, with a note of who saved

3
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:20,084
it. You read the author line. The CTO asks
the next question: how do you know that

4
00:00:20,124 --> 00:00:22,532
line is true? Say your answer out loud.

5
00:00:27,372 --> 00:00:32,574
For an unsigned commit the honest answer,
in the textbook's words, is "anyone with

6
00:00:32,614 --> 00:00:37,625
push access". A signature changes the
answer, but only to a precise and limited

7
00:00:37,665 --> 00:00:42,868
statement about a key. This video shows
what's signed, which program signs it, and

8
00:00:42,908 --> 00:00:47,791
how to set up and verify SSH signing on
your own machine without GitHub being

9
00:00:47,831 --> 00:00:52,778
involved at all. Keep that second question.
Its exact answer comes at the end.

10
00:00:54,618 --> 00:00:58,469
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:58,509 --> 00:01:03,764
You know the commit object from Part 1: a
tree, which is the snapshot of the files,

12
00:01:03,804 --> 00:01:08,294
then parents, author, committer and
message. You know SSH keys from the

13
00:01:08,334 --> 00:01:13,461
authentication videos: a private key that
never leaves your machine, and a public

14
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:16,523
key that you hand out.
This video joins the two.

15
00:01:16,863 --> 00:01:21,821
There are three parts. First the header
itself: where a signature is stored in a

16
00:01:21,861 --> 00:01:26,882
commit and in a tag. Second, the program:
Git does no cryptography, so you'll see

17
00:01:26,922 --> 00:01:31,505
exactly what it asks of an external
program for each of the three formats.

18
00:01:31,545 --> 00:01:36,003
Third, SSH signing end to end: a key,
three settings, a signed commit, a

19
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:41,001
verification that fails for an instructive
reason, the allowed-signers file that

20
00:01:41,041 --> 00:01:46,186
repairs it, signing by default, a signed
tag, and the error messages for a key that

21
00:01:46,226 --> 00:01:47,061
can't be used.

22
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:52,972
Everything here is Git, not GitHub. What
GitHub displays for a signature is the

23
00:01:53,012 --> 00:01:54,463
subject of video 139.

24
00:01:54,803 --> 00:01:59,413
A note on the transcripts. Two of the three
demos, signed-header and ssh-signing,

25
00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:03,834
create a fresh key pair inside the
sandbox, so they're volatile: the key, the

26
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:08,542
signatures and the commit IDs on your
machine will differ from the ones on screen.

27
00:02:10,382 --> 00:02:11,960
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:13,712
After this video you can:

29
00:02:14,052 --> 00:02:18,114
say which bytes of a commit a signature
covers and where the signature is stored;

30
00:02:18,454 --> 00:02:22,515
name the three signing formats Git
supports and the program each uses;

31
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:26,252
configure SSH signing and
sign a commit and a tag;

32
00:02:26,592 --> 00:02:29,747
verify a signature locally
with an allowed-signers file;

33
00:02:30,087 --> 00:02:33,649
explain the difference between "good
signature" and "trusted signer".

34
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:40,322
In one sentence. A Git signature is a
detached cryptographic signature over the

35
00:02:40,362 --> 00:02:45,504
bytes of one commit or one annotated tag,
produced by an external program and stored

36
00:02:45,544 --> 00:02:46,676
inside that object.

37
00:02:47,016 --> 00:02:51,865
Precisely. Git computes the object without
a signature, hands those bytes to a

38
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:57,130
signing program, and embeds the result. In
a commit the result is the gpgsig header.

39
00:02:57,170 --> 00:03:02,270
In a tag it is a block appended to the
message. That means an annotated tag, which

40
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:07,473
is an object of its own. Since the signature
is part of the object, it's covered by

41
00:03:07,513 --> 00:03:10,356
the object ID, the hash
that names the object.

42
00:03:10,696 --> 00:03:17,085
Quick quiz. You sign a commit that already
exists. Does its ID, A, stay the same, or

43
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:18,845
B, change? Your answer?

44
00:03:23,685 --> 00:03:28,710
B, it changes. Signing an existing commit
means writing a new commit, with a new ID.

45
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:33,595
And you can't attach a signature to history
after the fact without rewriting that

46
00:03:33,635 --> 00:03:35,765
history. Both come up in interviews.

47
00:03:36,105 --> 00:03:40,730
Don't confuse two options that differ
only in the case of one letter.

48
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:45,936
Capital -S signs with a key. Lower-case
-s adds the Signed-off-by text to the

49
00:03:45,976 --> 00:03:50,939
message. One is cryptography. The other
is a line of text anyone can type.

50
00:03:51,279 --> 00:03:54,873
Which program. The setting
gpg.format chooses.

51
00:03:55,213 --> 00:04:01,127
With openpgp, the default, the program is
gpg. The key is chosen by user.signingKey,

52
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:06,868
or else by the committer identity. Trust
is decided by your GnuPG keyring and its

53
00:04:06,908 --> 00:04:07,790
trust levels.

54
00:04:08,130 --> 00:04:12,504
With x509, available since Git
2.19, the program is gpgsm.

55
00:04:12,544 --> 00:04:18,440
The key is chosen the same way, and trust
is decided by the certificate chain.

56
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:23,454
With ssh, available since Git
2.34, the program is ssh-keygen.

57
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:29,764
The key is user.signingKey, which is a key
file, or a literal key with an agent, or

58
00:04:29,804 --> 00:04:35,847
else the command in gpg.ssh.defaultKeyCommand.
And trust is decided by one plain

59
00:04:35,887 --> 00:04:40,104
text file: the file named by
gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile.

60
00:04:40,444 --> 00:04:44,060
Each program can be replaced
through gpg.<format>.program.

61
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:49,038
The demonstration uses that to show you
the arguments without starting any real

62
00:04:49,078 --> 00:04:50,047
signing program.

63
00:04:50,387 --> 00:04:55,574
The rest of the course uses SSH signing,
for two reasons the textbook gives: you

64
00:04:55,614 --> 00:04:59,232
already have SSH keys, and
the trust file is plain text.

65
00:04:59,272 --> 00:05:04,655
OpenPGP and X.509 signing aren't run in the
labs, because both would start an agent

66
00:05:04,695 --> 00:05:05,178
process.

67
00:05:05,518 --> 00:05:10,726
Signing and verifying need different things.
This is the idea that the whole second

68
00:05:10,766 --> 00:05:14,393
half of the demonstration rests
on. Signing needs one key.

69
00:05:14,433 --> 00:05:19,641
Verifying needs a statement of whom you
trust. An SSH key has no owner written into

70
00:05:19,681 --> 00:05:24,953
it and there's no web of trust. So with SSH,
verification means: find this key in my

71
00:05:24,993 --> 00:05:29,822
list of allowed signers. Without a list,
Git doesn't start ssh-keygen at all.

72
00:05:30,162 --> 00:05:35,424
An allowed-signers line has three parts.
First a principal, which by convention is

73
00:05:35,464 --> 00:05:39,239
an email address. Then optional
options. Then a public key.

74
00:05:39,279 --> 00:05:43,378
The option namespaces="git"
restricts the key to Git signatures.

75
00:05:43,718 --> 00:05:47,734
The result letters. For scripts,
the format placeholder %G?

76
00:05:47,774 --> 00:05:53,439
gives one letter. G is a good signature:
the key is in the allowed-signers file and

77
00:05:53,479 --> 00:05:59,007
valid at the signature's time. B is a bad
signature: the object was altered after

78
00:05:59,047 --> 00:06:02,651
signing, or the key is listed
in the revocation file.

79
00:06:02,991 --> 00:06:08,149
U is a good signature with unknown validity:
the key isn't in the file, or outside

80
00:06:08,189 --> 00:06:13,347
its validity window. N is no signature,
and it's also printed, with an error, when

81
00:06:13,387 --> 00:06:18,291
no allowed-signers file is configured.
The manual lists X, Y, R and E as well.

82
00:06:18,331 --> 00:06:21,334
The SSH demos of the
chapter don't produce them.

83
00:06:23,174 --> 00:06:28,114
Analogy. A seal pressed onto one page of
a ledger. Because each page cites earlier

84
00:06:28,154 --> 00:06:33,216
pages by numbers computed from their content,
the seal vouches for the whole history

85
00:06:33,256 --> 00:06:33,824
behind it.

86
00:06:34,164 --> 00:06:39,504
Follow that through. The signed bytes include
the tree ID, so the seal covers every

87
00:06:39,544 --> 00:06:43,976
file. They include the parent IDs,
so the seal covers every ancestor.

88
00:06:44,016 --> 00:06:49,421
One signature on the newest commit vouches
for the content of everything it is built

89
00:06:49,461 --> 00:06:49,616
on.

90
00:06:49,956 --> 00:06:54,820
The analogy breaks where it matters most,
and the textbook says so in one sentence:

91
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:58,246
the seal says which ring was
used, not whose hand held it.

92
00:06:58,286 --> 00:07:03,150
A signature is evidence about a key. Whether
that key was in the hand of the person

93
00:07:03,190 --> 00:07:07,935
named in the author line is a different
question, and no cryptography answers it.

94
00:07:08,275 --> 00:07:13,355
So keep two phrases apart. "Good signature"
is mathematics: these bytes were signed

95
00:07:13,395 --> 00:07:18,291
by this key. "Trusted signer" is your
decision: this key is on my list, for this

96
00:07:18,331 --> 00:07:23,473
principal. In the demonstration you'll see
the same object go from "no signature" to

97
00:07:23,513 --> 00:07:27,544
"good signature" without a single
byte of the repository changing.

98
00:07:27,584 --> 00:07:30,074
What changed was your statement of trust.

99
00:07:31,914 --> 00:07:36,767
Two layers. The bracket marks the signed
bytes: the object without gpgsig.

100
00:07:36,807 --> 00:07:42,122
The outer box is what the commit ID is
computed from, and the signature is inside

101
00:07:42,162 --> 00:07:44,833
it. That's why adding one changes the ID.

102
00:07:45,173 --> 00:07:49,923
Read the root-cause line: signing needs one
key; verifying needs a statement of whom

103
00:07:49,963 --> 00:07:50,493
you trust.

104
00:07:52,333 --> 00:07:53,809
Step 1: the header.

105
00:07:54,149 --> 00:07:59,741
git commit -S is 🟢 SAFE: it adds an object
and moves your branch as any commit does.

106
00:07:59,781 --> 00:08:03,161
Predict: where in the object
will the signature be?

107
00:08:03,201 --> 00:08:04,234
Say it out loud.

108
00:08:09,074 --> 00:08:14,275
Between the committer line and the message.
The header is named gpgsig whatever the

109
00:08:14,315 --> 00:08:19,580
format. Here its content is an SSH signature.
Continuation lines begin with a space.

110
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:26,922
Step 2: what Git asks of the program.
Replay labs/run ch14b/signing-backends.

111
00:08:27,262 --> 00:08:31,096
The stand-in prints its arguments
and exits with an error.

112
00:08:31,136 --> 00:08:36,171
It signs nothing. And gpg.format isn't
set, so the default applies: OpenPGP.

113
00:08:36,511 --> 00:08:41,862
No user.signingKey is configured. How will
Git tell the program which key to use?

114
00:08:41,902 --> 00:08:43,259
Make your prediction.

115
00:08:48,099 --> 00:08:52,161
Read the arguments: the key was
selected by the committer identity.

116
00:08:52,201 --> 00:08:57,120
And read the first line, because you'll
meet it in real life: "gpg failed to sign

117
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:02,141
the data". That's the generic message for
"the signing program returned an error".

118
00:09:02,181 --> 00:09:06,855
No key for that identity, an agent that
can't ask for the passphrase, a wrong

119
00:09:06,895 --> 00:09:10,222
gpg.program: all produce it.
The commit wasn't written.

120
00:09:10,562 --> 00:09:15,020
X.509: another program
setting, the same arguments.

121
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:18,994
The SSH format, and still
no signing key configured.

122
00:09:19,034 --> 00:09:22,597
Will Git fall back to the
committer identity again?

123
00:09:22,637 --> 00:09:23,587
Say yes or no.

124
00:09:25,927 --> 00:09:31,402
It won't. The SSH backend has no default
key. With a key, Git runs ssh-keygen -Y

125
00:09:31,442 --> 00:09:35,677
sign with the namespace git, and
passes the payload as a file.

126
00:09:36,017 --> 00:09:40,758
A misspelled format is refused before
anything else happens, and the last commit is

127
00:09:40,798 --> 00:09:41,795
still the old one.

128
00:09:42,135 --> 00:09:49,252
Step 3: SSH signing end to end.
Replay labs/run ch14b/ssh-signing.

129
00:09:49,292 --> 00:09:50,879
Volatile again.

130
00:09:51,219 --> 00:09:54,586
A key pair without a
passphrase, inside the sandbox.

131
00:09:54,626 --> 00:10:00,025
Say this aloud: this is a lab key. A real
signing key has a passphrase and lives in

132
00:10:00,065 --> 00:10:01,925
an agent or a hardware token.

133
00:10:02,265 --> 00:10:06,990
git config set is 🟡 CAUTION: it changes
one configuration file, which has no

134
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:12,069
history. In the lab, "global" is the
sandbox's isolated configuration, never your

135
00:10:12,109 --> 00:10:12,320
own.

136
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:16,666
Look at the file name: user.signingKey
names the public key file.

137
00:10:16,706 --> 00:10:20,339
ssh-keygen finds the private
key beside it, or in an agent.

138
00:10:20,679 --> 00:10:24,948
A signed commit, read as an object.
The same header as in step 1.

139
00:10:25,288 --> 00:10:28,741
You signed this commit thirty
seconds ago with your own key.

140
00:10:28,781 --> 00:10:31,593
What will git verify-commit
say? Say it out loud.

141
00:10:36,433 --> 00:10:41,446
An error, exit status 1, "No signature",
and the letter N, for a commit that visibly

142
00:10:41,486 --> 00:10:45,837
has a gpgsig header. This is the
root-cause box from the diagram section.

143
00:10:45,877 --> 00:10:49,506
Git was never told whom to trust,
so it didn't verify at all.

144
00:10:49,846 --> 00:10:53,900
One line in a text file: principal,
namespace option, public key.

145
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:59,191
Now the same object verifies: a good "git"
signature for that principal, exit status

146
00:10:59,231 --> 00:11:04,167
0. Nothing in the repository changed. And
in the two-commit log, only the newer

147
00:11:04,207 --> 00:11:08,198
commit carries a signature line.
The older one was never signed.

148
00:11:08,538 --> 00:11:18,030
The placeholders for scripts: %G? for the
result letter, %GS for the signer, %GT for

149
00:11:18,070 --> 00:11:21,775
the trust level, %GK for the key.

150
00:11:22,115 --> 00:11:27,715
Try it now, thirty seconds,
in a repository of your own.

151
00:11:27,755 --> 00:11:32,850
Run git log -3 --format='%h
%G? %s'. It only reads.

152
00:11:32,890 --> 00:11:35,368
Which letters do you get?

153
00:11:37,708 --> 00:11:42,505
With no signing configured, it's most often
N three times: no signature, or, with an

154
00:11:42,545 --> 00:11:47,284
error line above it, a signature that Git
couldn't check without an allowed-signers

155
00:11:47,324 --> 00:11:50,508
file. Any other letter is in
the list you heard earlier.

156
00:11:50,848 --> 00:11:55,623
With commit.gpgSign true, every
commit is signed without -S.

157
00:11:55,663 --> 00:12:00,678
--no-gpg-sign opts one commit
out, and the log shows it with N.

158
00:12:01,018 --> 00:12:05,846
tag.gpgSign is true. The next
command creates a tag with -m only.

159
00:12:05,886 --> 00:12:09,590
What kind of tag will it
be? Make your prediction.

160
00:12:17,430 --> 00:12:21,302
A signed tag. git tag is 🟢 SAFE:
it adds an object and a ref.

161
00:12:21,342 --> 00:12:26,304
In a tag the signature follows the message.
git verify-tag and git tag -v both

162
00:12:26,344 --> 00:12:30,537
report a good signature. The second
also prints the tag's content.

163
00:12:30,577 --> 00:12:35,410
All three verification commands exit
non-zero on anything but a trusted good

164
00:12:35,450 --> 00:12:37,655
signature, so they work in scripts.

165
00:12:37,995 --> 00:12:40,642
With GIT_TRACE you see
the programs Git starts.

166
00:12:40,982 --> 00:12:46,072
One to sign. Two steps to verify: find
which principal the key belongs to, then

167
00:12:46,112 --> 00:12:48,670
verify the signature for that principal.

168
00:12:49,010 --> 00:12:51,296
Step 4: when the key cannot be used.

169
00:12:51,636 --> 00:12:56,773
Three failures, three messages. The private
key is missing: "No private key found".

170
00:12:56,813 --> 00:13:01,452
The path is wrong: "Couldn't load public
key". A literal key with no agent:

171
00:13:01,492 --> 00:13:06,068
"Couldn't get agent socket". In each
case the commit object isn't written.

172
00:13:06,108 --> 00:13:10,996
Remember that commit.gpgSign is true in
this sandbox: a broken key blocks every

173
00:13:11,036 --> 00:13:11,433
commit.

174
00:13:13,273 --> 00:13:14,921
Five mistakes to watch for.

175
00:13:15,261 --> 00:13:18,396
"error: gpg failed to sign
the data" and no idea why.

176
00:13:18,436 --> 00:13:23,247
Root cause: the message only says that the
signing program returned an error; the

177
00:13:23,287 --> 00:13:26,602
format, the key and the program
each have to be checked.

178
00:13:26,942 --> 00:13:32,684
A signed commit shows "No signature".
Root cause: gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile is

179
00:13:32,724 --> 00:13:37,075
unset or the file is missing,
so Git does not verify at all.

180
00:13:37,415 --> 00:13:43,344
Pointing user.signingKey at a path that
does not exist, or expecting SSH signing to

181
00:13:43,384 --> 00:13:48,449
pick a key by identity. Root cause:
the SSH backend has no default key.

182
00:13:48,789 --> 00:13:52,813
Switching on commit.gpgSign and
then losing access to the key.

183
00:13:52,853 --> 00:13:58,123
Root cause: with the setting on and no
working key, every commit fails, including

184
00:13:58,163 --> 00:14:03,367
the one you need during an incident; know
--no-gpg-sign, and whether your server

185
00:14:03,407 --> 00:14:04,612
accepts the result.

186
00:14:04,952 --> 00:14:10,779
Confusing -s with -S. Root cause: one adds
a text trailer to the message, the other

187
00:14:10,819 --> 00:14:13,747
adds a cryptographic header to the object.

188
00:14:15,587 --> 00:14:20,259
Now, out of the lab. A team that serves
an inference gateway decides to sign

189
00:14:20,299 --> 00:14:24,909
commits. Each engineer generates a
signing key and sets the three settings.

190
00:14:24,949 --> 00:14:29,746
On the first day everyone's own commits
verify on their own machine and nobody

191
00:14:29,786 --> 00:14:34,458
else's do: each person has an allowed-signers
file with one line, their own.

192
00:14:34,798 --> 00:14:38,153
The fix is the prevention
line of the root-cause box.

193
00:14:38,193 --> 00:14:43,022
The team keeps one allowed-signers file
in the repository, with one line per

194
00:14:43,062 --> 00:14:48,211
engineer, reviewed like code, and each
clone points gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile at

195
00:14:48,251 --> 00:14:53,528
it. From then on a release script can run
git verify-tag and trust the exit status.

196
00:14:53,868 --> 00:14:58,880
Then the first key rotation. An engineer
replaces their line with the new key, and

197
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:03,624
their old commits turn from G to U. The
textbook's rule: rotation adds, never

198
00:15:03,664 --> 00:15:05,596
replaces. Both keys stay listed.

199
00:15:07,436 --> 00:15:14,042
Your turn. Do Lab 24.1, "SSH signing
locally", in lab-manual/m24-signing-local.md.

200
00:15:14,082 --> 00:15:18,744
It runs in labs/shell with a
key generated in the sandbox.

201
00:15:18,784 --> 00:15:22,310
Your key and IDs will
differ from the video.

202
00:15:22,650 --> 00:15:27,230
Before you run each verification command,
predict the result letter and the exit

203
00:15:27,270 --> 00:15:31,849
status. In particular, predict what
verification says before the allowed-signers

204
00:15:31,889 --> 00:15:33,119
file exists and after.

205
00:15:33,459 --> 00:15:39,376
The challenge is Exercise 24.1,
"Covered or not covered?", in

206
00:15:39,416 --> 00:15:42,110
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

207
00:15:45,950 --> 00:15:50,120
Answer out loud first. A strong answer names
what each option adds and in which part

208
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:54,281
of the commit object it sits, and says which
of the two is evidence of anything and

209
00:15:54,321 --> 00:15:58,491
of what. It mentions that the signature is
inside the object and what that means for

210
00:15:58,531 --> 00:15:59,193
the commit ID.

211
00:15:59,533 --> 00:16:03,688
Be ready for the follow-up about
signing every commit on a shared branch

212
00:16:03,728 --> 00:16:08,350
retroactively: reason from the fact that
signing an existing commit writes a new

213
00:16:08,390 --> 00:16:13,187
commit, and work out what that does to every
descendant and to everyone who has the

214
00:16:13,227 --> 00:16:13,886
old history.

215
00:16:15,726 --> 00:16:19,659
Let's land this. The CTO asked how
you know the author line is true.

216
00:16:19,699 --> 00:16:24,567
For an unsigned commit, you don't. For a
signed one, you know which key signed those

217
00:16:24,607 --> 00:16:27,137
bytes, and whether that
key is on your list.

218
00:16:27,477 --> 00:16:29,137
You should now be able to say:

219
00:16:29,477 --> 00:16:33,991
A signature covers the bytes of one commit
or tag without the signature, and is

220
00:16:34,031 --> 00:16:38,718
stored inside the object: the gpgsig header
in a commit, a block after the message

221
00:16:38,758 --> 00:16:39,237
in a tag.

222
00:16:39,577 --> 00:16:45,808
Git does no cryptography; gpg.format
selects gpg, gpgsm or ssh-keygen.

223
00:16:46,148 --> 00:16:52,765
SSH signing needs gpg.format,
user.signingKey and, for verification,

224
00:16:52,805 --> 00:16:55,408
gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile.

225
00:16:55,748 --> 00:16:59,396
Without an allowed-signers file, a
signed commit reports "No signature".

226
00:16:59,736 --> 00:17:04,480
A good signature is a fact about a key; a
trusted signer is your decision about that

227
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:04,708
key.

228
00:17:06,548 --> 00:17:11,755
